net: Riseup VPN, Tor and a kill switch
======================================

For Gentoo with OpenRC. Set up as root with:

    su -c '~/dotfiles/install.sh net'

then reboot (or: rc-service vpnfw start && rc-service vpn start).


What happens at boot
--------------------

1. vpnfw (boot runlevel, before the network is up) loads the kill
   switch: no IPv6 at all, and IPv4 only from root to Riseup's
   servers. Nothing else can get out, so nothing leaks while the rest
   starts.
2. vpn (default runlevel) picks a server. Zones beat speed: the zones
   are in /etc/vpn/countries (white, then gray, then black). In the
   first zone with any working server, up to 3 random servers are
   speed-tested through the tunnel (5 MB or 6 seconds each, from the
   mirrors in /etc/vpn/mirrors), and the fastest one is used. Nothing
   depends on where you are.
3. OpenVPN connects to that server. The clock is set from two mirrors'
   answers through the tunnel, when they agree. If the certificate
   expires within 14 days or the server list is a week old, both are
   renewed, through the tunnel.
4. Tor starts, after OpenVPN, so it goes out through the VPN.
5. /etc/resolv.conf says "nameserver 127.0.0.1" and is locked (chattr
   +i): every DNS lookup goes to Tor. DNS sent to any other server is
   sent to Tor too.

If the tunnel drops, vpn connects to the same server again; if that
fails 3 times it picks again once. If it still doesn't work, or it
drops 3 times in 10 minutes, it stops and you stay offline: your
prompt shows VPN, and you choose what to do (vpn).

A black country is only used when no white or gray server works, and
then everything goes through Tor (level 3).


Levels
------

    0        your own IP, DNS from Cloudflare (1.1.1.1)
    1        VPN, Cloudflare DNS through the VPN
    2        VPN, DNS through Tor (the usual one)
    3        VPN, and every TCP connection goes through Tor; anything
             else (UDP) is dropped
    sandbox  your own IP, but only programs started with "vpn run" (and
             Tor) can reach the network; nothing else goes out by
             accident
    offline  nothing goes out

"vpn level 3" switches until reboot (between 1, 2 and 3 without a new
connection); "vpn default 3" sets the level used at boot. In every
level Tor runs for proxychains and the browser, IPv6 is off, nothing
comes in that wasn't asked for, and .onion addresses work.

The prompt shows VPN when something is wrong, or the level when it
isn't 2 (L0, L1, L3, SANDBOX).


Commands
--------

    vpn status          state and level (anyone)
    vpn status -v       server, country, Tor, DNS, firewall, clock
    vpn                 a menu with everything
    vpn level L         0 1 2 3 sandbox offline, until reboot
    vpn default L       the level at boot
    vpn retry           pick a server again and connect
    vpn refresh         a new server list and certificate from Riseup
    vpn lan on|off      reach your local network (router, printer) or
                        not; off by default, outgoing only
    vpn firewall-off    no kill switch at all, until vpn firewall-on
    vpn run PROGRAM     a program that may go online in the sandbox
    proxychains PROGRAM run a program through Tor (port 9050)

Every command but "vpn status" and "vpn run" asks for your own
password (not root's): vpnauth checks it and runs vpn as root. You
must be in group vpnctl (install.sh net puts you there; log in again).
vpn run needs group vpnallow, also set up by install.sh net.

The browser: set its SOCKS5 proxy to 127.0.0.1 port 9150, with DNS
through the proxy. That port has its own Tor circuits, so sites can't
link the browser to what proxychains runs by their exit.

When the VPN can't be reached at all, "vpn refresh" offers to open the
firewall for root only, to DNS and HTTPS, to fetch a new list and
certificate (your ISP and your router's DNS see that you ask Riseup;
they see that you use Riseup anyway). When Riseup's HTTPS certificate
can't be checked, it tells you and asks whether to go on without the
check.


Privacy
-------

- Your ISP sees only that you use Riseup. Riseup sees your IP and that
  you use Tor, not what you do in it. Tor's first relay sees Riseup's
  address, not yours.
- dhcpcd sends no hostname and uses a random MAC address each time it
  starts (its anonymous mode, RFC 7844). The hostname is "host" and
  the clock is in UTC, so programs can't give away either.
- Nothing is logged for long: the status, OpenVPN's and Tor's warnings
  are in /run/vpn, which is gone after a reboot.
- The server list and certificate are Riseup's anonymous ones: no
  account.

Limits: proxychains can't wrap statically linked programs. In level 3
UDP doesn't work (voice calls, some games). Programs can still learn
things about you by other means (a browser's fingerprint).


Files
-----

    net/vpn           /usr/local/bin/vpn
    net/vpnauth.c     /usr/local/bin/vpnauth (setuid root, group vpnctl)
    net/vpnfw.initd   /etc/init.d/vpnfw (boot)
    net/vpn.initd     /etc/init.d/vpn (default)
    net/openvpn.conf  /etc/vpn/openvpn.conf
    net/countries     /etc/vpn/countries: edit here, install.sh net
    net/mirrors       /etc/vpn/mirrors
    net/torrc         /etc/tor/torrc (with the old User, DataDirectory)
    net/proxychains.conf  /etc/proxychains.conf
    net/sysctl.conf   /etc/sysctl.d/vpn.conf (IPv6 off)

From Riseup, in /etc/vpn: gateways (one server per line: IP, country,
place, host, ports), ca.crt, client.crt, client.key. lan exists when
the local network is on, level holds the boot level.

The way the list and certificate are fetched comes from BarbossHack's
RiseupVPN-OpenVPN (generate.sh); vpn is our own POSIX sh version.
